# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is git-ignored; this file is not, so it must never carry a
# real key.
#
# One variable, used by one thing: the live integration tests in
# tests/MassiveDotNet.IntegrationTests, which call the real Massive API and are marked
# `Category=Integration`. Every other test in the suite runs without it.
#
# CI never has this. It is not a secret that CI is missing by oversight -- rule 13 makes it
# structural: the offline suite is what CI runs, no workflow may reference a Massive credential,
# and a CI job fails the build if one ever does. Live tests are committed and compiled in CI so
# public API drift breaks the build, but they are excluded from the run by category.
#
# Without a key, `dotnet test` skips the live tier and says so, naming this variable. That is
# honest feedback to a person; in CI the same tests are never invoked at all, because a skip
# reports into the summary and reads as green while a project CI never runs makes no claim.
#
# Read by LiveCredentials.cs: the environment variable first, then this file at the repository
# root. The key is never written to disk by the SDK, never logged, and never rendered into an
# exception (rule 11).
MASSIVE_API_KEY=$YOUR_API_KEY
